In most of the other cases, you can get the intrinsic by eating
the dragon's corpse.  This sort of implies an inherent property
independent of the scales -- much like blue jellies are immune
to cold because they *are* cold, poisonous creatures are poison
resistant, and so on.  Most NetHack creatures that have any
kind of built-in special-damage-type attack for which there is
a corresponding resistance are themselves resistant to it.
In many cases you can acquire this resistance by eating them.

Silver dragons apparently only have reflection because of their
shiny silver scales.  I guess they aren't magical at all really
(except in the ways that ALL dragons are magical, e.g., being
able to fly despite a preposterous mass-to-wingspan ratio and
less speed than any real-world flying creature).

It makes sense to me that baby dragons would hatch with
few or no scales.  Baby birds hatch with no feathers, 
and I think there are reptiles that hatch without (most 
of) their scales.  Many mammals are born with little or
no hair.

The special cases, IMO, are gray and yellow.  They *have*
an intrinsic special immunity, but you can't get it by
eating their corpse.  In the case of gray dragons, this
is for play balance reasons (intrinsic magic resistance
would be overpowered).  I'm not sure what the reasoning
is for not allowing the player to acquire intrinsic acid
resistance (other than via polyself).

